;; Docs for std/test/gen.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "gen.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Generators of test values that draw through a recorder, so that a failing case shrinks by replaying fewer and smaller draws.")
  (see Gen draw gen-let Arbitrary gen-sample)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std test gen))

    (type (: Suit (Union Hearts Spades Clubs Diamonds)))
    (type (: Card (Record (: rank int) (: suit Suit))))

    (: gen-card (Gen Card))
    (def gen-card
      (gen-let ((rank (gen-int-range 1 13))
                (suit (gen-element (list Hearts Spades Clubs Diamonds))))
        (Card (rank rank) (suit suit))))

    (impl (Arbitrary Card) (defun (arbitrary) gen-card))

    (gen-sample gen-card)                ; one card
    (gen-samples (gen-list gen-int) 5)   ; five lists, small ones first
    """)
  (reference """
    A @code{(Gen T)} makes values of type T. Property tests in
    @code{(std test prop)} use generators, but this module does not import
    @code{(std test)}. A program can use the generators without the test
    runner, for example to make fixtures.

    @strong{The recorder.} A generator does not read a @code{RandomSource}
    itself. It asks a @code{Choices} for numbers with @code{draw}. A
    @code{Choices} has one of two modes:

    @itemlist{
      @item{@strong{Recording} (@code{recording-choices}): it draws each
        number from a random source and records it.}
      @item{@strong{Replaying} (@code{replaying-choices}): it answers the
        numbers of a vector, in order. When the vector is used up, each
        further draw answers 0.}
    }

    @code{draw} records the number after it puts it into its bound, not the
    raw random number. So a recorded vector replays to the same value, and a
    changed vector replays to a value that is still in range.

    @strong{Smaller draws give simpler values.} @code{(std test prop)}
    shrinks a failing case: it makes the recorded vector smaller and replays
    it. This gives a simpler value only when each generator obeys this rule:
    0 is the simplest choice at every draw, and a larger draw gives a value
    farther from the simplest. Because a used-up vector answers 0, a shorter
    vector also gives a simpler value. Thus no generator needs a shrinker of
    its own. Every generator in this module obeys the rule:

    @itemlist{
      @item{A number generator gives the value nearest 0 at a draw of 0:
        the lower bound of a range above 0, the upper bound of a range
        below 0, and 0 itself otherwise.}
      @item{@code{gen-list} draws "one more element?" before each element,
        and 0 means "no more". So deleting draws makes the list shorter.}
      @item{@code{gen-one-of}, @code{gen-frequency} and
        @code{gen-element} take their first alternative at 0. Put the
        simplest alternative first.}
      @item{@code{gen-recursive} takes the base case at 0.}
      @item{@code{gen-bool} gives #f, @code{gen-option} None,
        @code{gen-result} Ok, @code{gen-char} #\a, @code{gen-string} the
        empty string, and @code{gen-shuffle} the order it was given.}
    }

    A generator made from these with @code{gen-map}, @code{gen-bind},
    @code{gen-let} and the other combinators obeys the rule too.

    @strong{A generator of your own.} Build it from the generators above
    when you can: it then obeys the rule without more work. A generator
    made with @code{make-gen} that calls @code{draw} itself must obey the
    rule on its own:

    @itemlist{
      @item{Map a draw of 0 to the simplest value: the origin or identity
        of the domain, such as 0, the empty list or the base case. A range
        that does not hold 0 starts at its end nearest 0, so add the draw to
        the lower bound, @code{(+ lo (draw c n))}, for a range above 0.}
      @item{Make a larger draw give a value farther from the simplest. For
        a range across 0, draw the sign first, positive at 0, and then the
        distance from 0, as @code{gen-int-range} does. A draw over the whole
        range, added to @code{lo}, makes the most negative value the
        simplest, and shrinking then goes to it.}
      @item{When a draw chooses between kinds of value, put the simplest
        kind at 0, and make a kind that is a shortcut to a value, such as
        an edge value, cost at least as many draws as the other way to the
        same value. Shrinking prefers fewer draws, and would otherwise keep
        the shortcut.}
      @item{Draw a "one more?" choice, 0 for no, before each element of a
        collection, so that deleting draws removes elements.}
    }

    A generator that breaks the rule still works: its values are as random
    as before. Only its failing cases shrink less well.

    @strong{Sizes.} A @code{Choices} has a size, which @code{choices-size}
    gives. Numbers, lists and recursive values grow with it. A property's
    first runs have small sizes and its last runs large ones, so small
    cases are tried first. @code{gen-sized} reads the size, and
    @code{gen-resize} changes it.

    @strong{Edge values.} The number and text generators give values that
    often find bugs: @code{int-max}, -1, NaN when asked for, surrogate
    pairs, combining characters. An edge of an integer is recorded as the
    draws that the full-range way to the same value would record. So
    shrinking can change the draw that chose the edge, keep the value, and
    then make the value smaller.

    @strong{Discarded runs.} A generator can give up: @code{gen-such-that}
    after 100 tries, and any generator after 10000 draws. It then calls
    @code{discard!}, which marks the @code{Choices} and raises an exception.
    @code{(std test prop)} does not count a discarded run.

    @strong{Gen is not a Monad.} The callback of the bind of a
    @code{Monad} can be a bjoroutine, but a generator runs inside an
    ordinary function. Use @code{gen-let} to draw values in order, or
    @code{gen-bind}.

    @strong{Defaults by type.} The trait @code{Arbitrary} gives a generator
    for a type. @code{(for-all ((: xs (List int))) ...)} uses it. An
    instance is only a generator, because shrinking needs nothing more.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The recorder
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Choices
  (summary "Where a generator gets its numbers: from a random source, recorded, or from a recorded vector, replayed.")
  (see recording-choices replaying-choices draw choices-draws)
  (reference """
    A @code{Choices} records every number that it answers, in both modes.
    After a replay, the record is the part of the vector that the replay
    used, followed by the zeros it answered after the vector was used up.
    A @code{Choices} also holds the size of the run, and whether the run
    was discarded.

    A @code{Choices} is for one run of a generator. Make a new one for each
    run.
    """))

(:doc recording-choices
  (summary "A Choices that draws from a random source and records each number.")
  (arg rng "The source to draw from.")
  (arg size "The size of the run. Numbers, lists and recursive values grow with it.")
  (returns "A new Choices, with nothing recorded.")
  (see replaying-choices choices-draws)
  (example """
    (def c (recording-choices (make-random/seed64 3UL) 60))
    (def v (gen-run (gen-list gen-int) c))
    (choices-draws c)   ; the draws that replay to v
    """))

(:doc replaying-choices
  (summary "A Choices that answers the numbers of a vector in order, and 0 after the vector is used up.")
  (arg draws "The numbers to answer, usually from @code{choices-draws}.")
  (arg size "The size of the run. Give the size of the recording, or a replay can give a different value.")
  (returns "A new Choices, with nothing recorded.")
  (see recording-choices choices-draws)
  (example """
    (gen-run g (replaying-choices (choices-draws c) 60))   ; the value again
    (gen-run g (replaying-choices (vec-empty) 60))         ; the simplest value of g
    """)
  (reference """
    A number of the vector is put into the bound of its draw. So a vector
    that was changed, for example by shrinking, still gives a value in
    range.
    """))

(:doc draw
  (summary "A number from 0, included, to bound, not included. Every generator is made from this.")
  (arg c "The choices to draw from.")
  (arg bound "The bound. 0 means every ulong.")
  (returns "A number in [0, bound). A replay answers the next number of its vector, put into the bound, or 0 when the vector is used up.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the run drew 10000 numbers before. The run is then discarded: see @code{discard!}.")
  (see choices-size discard!)
  (example """
    ;; A die. A draw of 0 gives 1, the simplest value.
    (def gen-die (make-gen (fun (c) (+ 1 (cast System.Int32 (draw c 6UL))))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The number is recorded after it is put into the bound. Map 0 to the
    simplest value, and larger numbers to values farther from it, so that
    shrinking makes values simpler.

    A recording puts a random number into the bound with a remainder, so a
    bound that is not a power of 2 makes small numbers slightly more likely.
    """))

(:doc choices-size
  (summary "The size of the run.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "The size given when the choices were made, or the size that @code{gen-resize} set.")
  (see gen-sized gen-resize))

(:doc choices-draws
  (summary "Every number that the choices answered, in order.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "The record. Replayed with @code{replaying-choices}, at the same size, it gives the same value.")
  (see replaying-choices)
  (reference """
    After a replay, the record is the part of the vector that was used, and
    a 0 for each draw after the vector was used up.
    """))

(:doc choices-discarded?
  (summary "Whether the run was discarded.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "#t after @code{discard!}.")
  (see discard!)
  (reference """
    A discarded run ends with an exception. The code that ran the generator
    examines this flag, not the exception, to tell a discard from a
    failure.
    """))

(:doc discard!
  (summary "Marks the run as discarded and leaves it with an exception.")
  (arg c "The choices of the run.")
  (arg why "Why the run is discarded. It is the message of the exception, after @code{discarded: }.")
  (returns "Nothing: it always raises. The result type fits any position.")
  (raises ArgumentException "Always.")
  (see choices-discarded? gen-such-that)
  (reference """
    Use it in a generator that can not make a good value from its draws.
    @code{(std test prop)} does not count a discarded run, and fails a
    property when more than 10 times its runs are discarded.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Generators, and running one
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Gen
  (summary "A generator of values of type %a.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (see make-gen gen-run gen-let Arbitrary)
  (reference """
    A generator is a function from a @code{Choices} to a value. Make one
    from the generators and combinators of this module, or with
    @code{make-gen}.
    """))

(:doc make-gen
  (summary "A generator from a function that draws from a Choices.")
  (arg f "The function. It gets the choices of the run, and answers a value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see draw gen-run)
  (example """
    (: gen-even (Gen int))
    (def gen-even
      (make-gen (fun (c) (* 2 (cast System.Int32 (draw c 1000UL))))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The function must get all its numbers from the choices, through
    @code{draw} or other generators. It must map a draw of 0 to the
    simplest value, or shrinking does not make its values simpler.
    """))

(:doc gen-run
  (summary "Runs a generator with the choices of a run.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (arg c "The choices.")
  (returns "The value that g makes from the draws of c.")
  (see make-gen gen-sample)
  (example """
    (gen-run gen-int (replaying-choices (vec-empty) 50))   ; 0
    """))

(:doc gen-sample
  (summary "One value of a generator.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (key size "The size of the run.")
  (key rng "The source to draw from. A new source each call when not given.")
  (returns "A value that g makes.")
  (see gen-samples)
  (example """
    (gen-sample (gen-list gen-int))
    (gen-sample gen-string #:size 5 #:rng (make-random/seed64 1UL))
    """))

(:doc gen-samples
  (summary "Values of a generator, at sizes that grow as the runs of a property do.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (arg n "How many values.")
  (key size "The size of the last value.")
  (key rng "The source to draw from. A new source each call when not given.")
  (returns "n values. The first is made at size 1 and the last at size, with sizes evenly between.")
  (see gen-sample)
  (example """
    (gen-samples (gen-list gen-int) 20 #:rng (make-random/seed64 1UL))
    """)
  (reference """
    Each value draws from its own source, split from rng. The same seed
    gives the same values.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Combinators
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-pure
  (summary "A generator that always makes x and draws nothing.")
  (arg x "The value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-element))

(:doc gen-map
  (summary "A generator whose values are those of another, with a function applied.")
  (arg f "The function.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (returns "A generator of the results of f.")
  (see gen-bind gen-let)
  (example """
    (def gen-even (gen-map (fun (n) (* 2 n)) gen-int))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{gen-map} applies a function to values. The generator of a prelude
    @code{Map} is @code{gen-map-of}.
    """))

(:doc gen-bind
  (summary "A generator that draws a value, and then draws from the generator that a function makes from it.")
  (arg g "The first generator.")
  (arg f "A function from the first value to the next generator. It must be an ordinary function, not a bjoroutine.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-let gen-map)
  (example """
    ;; A list and an index into it.
    (gen-bind (gen-such-that (fun (xs) (not (list-empty? xs))) (gen-list gen-int))
              (fun (xs) (gen-map (fun (i) (Tuple xs i))
                                 (gen-int-range 0 (- (list-length xs) 1)))))
    """)
  (reference """
    Both generators draw from the same choices, so shrinking makes both
    values simpler. @code{gen-let} writes the same thing with names.
    """))

(:doc gen-let
  (summary "A generator that draws each binding in order, as let* binds, and makes the value of the body.")
  (form (gen-let ((name gen) ...) body ...))
  (arg name "The name of the value.")
  (arg gen "The generator of the value. It can use the names of the bindings before it.")
  (rest body "The forms that make the value from the names. At least one form.")
  (see gen-bind gen-map)
  (example """
    (: gen-card (Gen Card))
    (def gen-card
      (gen-let ((rank (gen-int-range 1 13))
                (suit (gen-element (list Hearts Spades Clubs Diamonds))))
        (Card (rank rank) (suit suit))))

    ;; A later generator uses an earlier value.
    (gen-let ((n (gen-int-range 0 10))
              (xs (gen-list-of-length n gen-int)))
      (Tuple n xs))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{Gen} is not a @code{Monad}, so @code{(do ...)} does not make
    generators. @code{gen-let} is the way to draw values in order. All the
    bindings draw from the same choices.
    """))

(:doc gen-such-that
  (summary "A generator of the values of g for which pred holds.")
  (arg pred "The condition.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (returns "A generator that runs g until pred holds.")
  (see discard!)
  (example """
    (gen-such-that even? gen-int)
    """)
  (reference """
    After 100 values for which pred does not hold, the run is discarded.
    A condition that few values satisfy tests little, so a property whose
    runs are discarded too often fails. Prefer a generator that makes good
    values directly, for example @code{(gen-map (fun (n) (* 2 n)) gen-int)}
    instead of @code{(gen-such-that even? gen-int)}.
    """))

(:doc gen-one-of
  (summary "A generator that makes a value with one of the generators, each equally likely.")
  (arg gens "The generators. The first is the simplest: a draw of 0 chooses it.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When gens is empty.")
  (see gen-frequency gen-element)
  (example """
    (gen-one-of (list (gen-pure 0) (gen-int-range 1 9) gen-int))
    """))

(:doc gen-frequency
  (summary "A generator that makes a value with one of the generators, chosen by weight.")
  (arg choices "Pairs of a weight and a generator. A generator is chosen with a probability of its weight divided by the sum of the weights. The first is the simplest.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the sum of the weights is not above 0.")
  (see gen-one-of)
  (example """
    (gen-frequency (list (Tuple 1 (gen-pure None))
                         (Tuple 3 (gen-map Some gen-int))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A generator with a weight of 0 is never chosen. Give no weight less
    than 0.
    """))

(:doc gen-element
  (summary "A generator that makes one of the values, each equally likely.")
  (arg xs "The values. The first is the simplest: a draw of 0 chooses it.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When xs is empty.")
  (see gen-one-of gen-shuffle)
  (example """
    (gen-element (list Hearts Spades Clubs Diamonds))
    """))

(:doc gen-sized
  (summary "A generator that a function makes from the size of the run.")
  (arg f "A function from the size to a generator.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see choices-size gen-resize)
  (example """
    ;; A list of exactly size elements.
    (gen-sized (fun (n) (gen-list-of-length n gen-int)))
    """))

(:doc gen-resize
  (summary "A generator that runs g at another size.")
  (arg n "The size for g.")
  (arg g "The generator.")
  (returns "The generator. After g, the size is again what it was.")
  (see gen-sized)
  (example """
    (gen-resize 5 (gen-list gen-int))   ; short lists at every size
    """))

(:doc gen-recursive
  (summary "A generator of a recursive type, which halves the size at each level so that its values end.")
  (arg base "The generator of the base case, such as a leaf.")
  (arg step "A function from a generator of the smaller parts to a generator of one level.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-sized)
  (example """
    (type (: Tree (Union Leaf (: Node Tree int Tree))))

    (: gen-tree (Gen Tree))
    (def gen-tree
      (gen-recursive (gen-pure Leaf)
                     (fun (self) (gen-let ((l self) (x gen-int) (r self))
                                   (Node l x r)))))
    """)
  (reference """
    At size 0 or less, it makes the base case. At a larger size, a draw
    chooses: a draw of 0 makes the base case, and the others call step with
    this generator at half the size. Thus a case shrinks toward the base
    case. The base case comes at one draw in four, and always when the size
    reaches 0.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Numbers
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-bool
  (summary "#t or #f, equally likely. #f is the simplest.")
  (see gen-element))

(:doc gen-int
  (summary "An int that grows with the size, with edge values now and then. 0 is the simplest.")
  (see gen-int-range gen-long)
  (reference """
    A first draw chooses the kind of value:

    @itemlist{
      @item{8 times in 10, a value from -size² to size². So a size of 100
        reaches 10000.}
      @item{1 time in 10, any int.}
      @item{1 time in 10, an edge: 0, 1, -1, @code{int-max},
        @code{int-min}, 255, 256 or 65535.}
    }

    An edge is recorded as the draws for "any int" that give it. So
    shrinking changes the kind of an edge to "any int", keeps the value, and
    can then make the value smaller.
    """))

(:doc gen-int-range
  (summary "An int from lo to hi, both included, each equally likely.")
  (arg lo "The smallest value.")
  (arg hi "The largest value. Not less than lo.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When lo is above hi.")
  (see gen-int gen-long-range)
  (example """
    (gen-int-range 1 6)   ; a die
    """)
  (reference """
    The simplest value is the one nearest 0: lo when the range is above 0,
    hi when it is below 0, and 0 when the range contains 0. A range that
    contains 0 draws the sign first, then the distance from 0. So a value
    shrinks toward 0.
    """))

(:doc gen-long
  (summary "A long that grows with the size, with edge values now and then. 0 is the simplest.")
  (see gen-long-range gen-int)
  (reference """
    As @code{gen-int}, but over the longs. The edges are 0, 1, -1,
    @code{long-max}, @code{long-min}, @code{int-max}, @code{int-min} and
    2³².
    """))

(:doc gen-long-range
  (summary "A long from lo to hi, both included, each equally likely.")
  (arg lo "The smallest value.")
  (arg hi "The largest value. Not less than lo.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When lo is above hi.")
  (see gen-long gen-int-range)
  (reference """
    The simplest value is the one nearest 0, as for @code{gen-int-range}.
    The range can be all the longs.
    """))

(:doc gen-ulong
  (summary "A ulong that grows with the size, with edge values now and then. 0 is the simplest.")
  (see gen-long)
  (reference """
    8 times in 10, a value from 0 to size². 1 time in 10, any ulong. 1
    time in 10, an edge: 0, 1, the largest ulong, 2⁶³ or 2³². An edge is
    recorded as the draws for "any ulong" that give it, as for
    @code{gen-int}.
    """))

(:doc gen-double
  (summary "A double that grows with the size, with edge values now and then. 0.0 is the simplest.")
  (key special "#t to add NaN, positive infinity and negative infinity to the edges.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-int)
  (example """
    (gen-double)
    (gen-double #:special #t)   ; also NaN and the infinities
    """)
  (reference """
    9 times in 10, a whole part from -size² to size², with a fraction of 53
    bits added away from 0. 1 time in 10, an edge: 0.0, 1.0, -1.0, the
    largest and the smallest doubles, the smallest double above 0, and
    -0.0. NaN and the infinities find bugs, but many properties do not hold
    for them, so they come only with @code{#:special #t}.
    """))

(:doc gen-bigint
  (summary "A bigint: usually a long, and sometimes a value beyond the range of a long. 0 is the simplest.")
  (see gen-long)
  (reference """
    3 times in 4, a value of @code{gen-long}. 1 time in 4, that value
    multiplied by a number from 0 to 2⁶⁴, so most such values are beyond
    the range of a long.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Text
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-char
  (summary "Any Unicode scalar value, mostly printable ASCII, with characters that find bugs now and then. #\\a is the simplest.")
  (see gen-ascii-char gen-string)
  (reference """
    A first draw chooses the kind of character:

    @itemlist{
      @item{8 times in 10, a printable ASCII character, as
        @code{gen-ascii-char} makes.}
      @item{1 time in 10, a character that often finds bugs in text code:
        the NUL character, a combining acute accent, an emoji outside the
        BMP (two UTF-16 units), the Hebrew letter alef (right to left), the
        replacement character, a non-breaking space, the final sigma, or
        the capital I with a dot.}
      @item{1 time in 10, any scalar value: 0 to 0x10FFFF, without the
        surrogates.}
    }
    """))

(:doc gen-ascii-char
  (summary "A printable ASCII character, from space to ~. #\\a is the simplest.")
  (see gen-char gen-ascii-string)
  (reference """
    The 95 characters are put in an order that starts at #\a: the letters
    come before the digits and the punctuation. A case shrinks toward a
    letter, which is easy to read.
    """))

(:doc gen-string
  (summary "A string of gen-char characters, whose length grows with the size. The empty string is the simplest.")
  (see gen-ascii-string gen-string-of gen-char)
  (reference """
    The length is as for @code{gen-list}. Because of @code{gen-char}, it
    sometimes has surrogate pairs, combining characters and other
    characters that find bugs.
    """))

(:doc gen-ascii-string
  (summary "A string of printable ASCII characters, whose length grows with the size. The empty string is the simplest.")
  (see gen-string gen-ascii-char))

(:doc gen-string-of
  (summary "A string of the characters of a generator, whose length grows with the size.")
  (arg g "The generator of the characters.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty string is the simplest.")
  (see gen-string gen-list)
  (example """
    (gen-string-of (gen-element (string->list "ACGT")))   ; DNA
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Shapes
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-unit
  (summary "Always unit. It draws nothing.")
  (see gen-pure))

(:doc gen-option
  (summary "None, or Some value of g. None is the simplest.")
  (arg g "The generator of the value.")
  (returns "The generator. It makes None 1 time in 4.")
  (see gen-result))

(:doc gen-result
  (summary "Ok with a value of ga, or Err with a value of ge, equally likely. Ok is the simplest.")
  (arg ge "The generator of the error.")
  (arg ga "The generator of the value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-option))

(:doc gen-tuple2
  (summary "A pair of values, the first drawn first.")
  (arg ga "The generator of the first value.")
  (arg gb "The generator of the second value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-tuple3 gen-tuple4 gen-let))

(:doc gen-tuple3
  (summary "A tuple of three values, drawn in order.")
  (arg ga "The generator of the first value.")
  (arg gb "The generator of the second value.")
  (arg gc "The generator of the third value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-tuple2 gen-tuple4))

(:doc gen-tuple4
  (summary "A tuple of four values, drawn in order.")
  (arg ga "The generator of the first value.")
  (arg gb "The generator of the second value.")
  (arg gc "The generator of the third value.")
  (arg gd "The generator of the fourth value.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-tuple2 gen-tuple3)
  (reference """
    @code{Arbitrary} has no instance for tuples of four. Name this
    generator in the binding.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Sequences
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-list
  (summary "A list of values of g, whose length grows with the size. The empty list is the simplest.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-list-of-length gen-vec gen-array)
  (example """
    (gen-list gen-int)
    (gen-list (gen-int-range 0 20))
    """)
  (reference """
    Before each element, a draw tells whether there is one more element,
    and 0 tells that there is not. So shrinking makes a list shorter when it
    deletes draws, and ends it when it sets such a draw to 0.

    The length is 1 + size / 5 on average: 1 at size 0, and 21 at size 100.
    A list has at most 1000 elements, at any size.
    """))

(:doc gen-list-of-length
  (summary "A list of exactly n values of g.")
  (arg n "The length.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-list)
  (reference """
    The length does not shrink. Use @code{gen-list} when the length is part
    of what to test.
    """))

(:doc gen-vec
  (summary "A vec of values of g, as gen-list makes.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty vec is the simplest.")
  (see gen-list gen-array))

(:doc gen-array
  (summary "A new array of values of g, as gen-list makes.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty array is the simplest. Each value is a new array.")
  (see gen-list gen-vec))

(:doc gen-shuffle
  (summary "A permutation of the values. The order of xs is the simplest.")
  (arg xs "The values.")
  (returns "The generator.")
  (see gen-element)
  (reference """
    Fisher-Yates from the front: element i changes places with an element
    at i or after it, and a draw of 0 keeps it at i. So a case shrinks
    toward the order of xs.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Maps and sets
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc gen-map-of
  (summary "A prelude Map, made from a gen-list of keys and values.")
  (arg gk "The generator of the keys.")
  (arg gv "The generator of the values.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty map is the simplest.")
  (see gen-orderedmap gen-list)
  (reference """
    The map shrinks as its list of entries does. When the list has a key
    more than once, the last value wins. So the map can have fewer entries
    than the list.
    """))

(:doc gen-hashset
  (summary "A HashSet, made from a gen-list of its elements.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty set is the simplest.")
  (see gen-orderedset gen-list))

(:doc gen-orderedmap
  (summary "An OrderedMap, made from a gen-list of keys and values.")
  (arg gk "The generator of the keys.")
  (arg gv "The generator of the values.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty map is the simplest.")
  (see gen-map-of gen-list)
  (reference """
    When the list has a key more than once, the last value wins.
    """))

(:doc gen-orderedset
  (summary "An OrderedSet, made from a gen-list of its elements.")
  (arg g "The generator of the elements.")
  (returns "The generator. The empty set is the simplest.")
  (see gen-hashset gen-list))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Defaults by type
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Arbitrary
  (summary "The generator that a type has when a property names no generator.")
  (tparam %a "The type that the generator makes.")
  (see arbitrary Gen)
  (example """
    (impl (Arbitrary Card) (defun (arbitrary) gen-card))

    ;; In (std test prop): (: xs (List Card)) uses it.
    (for-all ((: xs (List Card)))
      (check (= (list-length (list-sort xs)) (list-length xs))))
    """)
  (reference """
    This module has instances for bool, int, long, ulong, bigint, double
    (without NaN and the infinities), char, string, void, @code{Option},
    @code{Result}, tuples of two and three, @code{List}, @code{Vec},
    @code{Map}, @code{HashSet}, @code{OrderedMap} and @code{OrderedSet}.
    An instance for a container needs instances for the types in it.

    An instance is only a generator. It has no shrinker, because shrinking
    replays smaller draws. A generator in an instance must obey the rule
    that smaller draws give simpler values.
    """))

(:doc arbitrary
  (summary "The generator of a type. The type of the result chooses the instance.")
  (returns "The generator of the instance for that type.")
  (see Arbitrary)
  (example """
    (def (: g (Gen (List int))) (arbitrary))
    (def (: cards (List Card)) (gen-samples (arbitrary) 10))
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes no arguments. Write the type where the result goes, for
    example with @code{(def (: name Type) ...)}.
    """))
